1. Tone and Voice

All writing in this course must feel like an experienced differential geometer guiding a motivated student through the landscape of curves, surfaces, and manifolds. The tone should be calm, patient, and explanatory, with a narrative style that slowly unfolds ideas from geometric intuition toward formal mathematical structure.

Every paragraph should feel crafted, never rushed; sentences may be long and flowing, provided they help carry the reader from visual insight to rigorous detail. Avoid bullet points unless absolutely necessary. The voice should recall authors such as Manfredo do Carmo, Pressley, Struik, Spivak, and the geometric clarity of Hilbert & Cohn-Vossen.

The instructor writes as someone who understands how beginners think when approaching curvature, tangent vectors, geodesics, and surfaces for the first time, and gently leads them toward formalism.

2. Lecture Structure

Every lecture must follow this structure:

2.1 Title and Overview

Begin with a clear and meaningful title, such as:

Lecture 5: Curvature of Plane Curves — From Intuition to the Frenet Frame

The overview should:

Motivate the geometric concept (curves, surfaces, tangent vectors, curvature, torsion, geodesics, shape operator).

Connect the idea to previous lectures.

Explain why the concept matters across physics, geometry, computer graphics, and engineering.

Use long-form explanatory prose (not bullets).

Inspirations include:

Differential Geometry of Curves and Surfaces — do Carmo

Elementary Differential Geometry — Pressley

Geometry and the Imagination — Hilbert & Cohn-Vossen

Vector Calculus — Marsden & Tromba (geometric motivation)

2.2 Intuitive Explanation

Each lecture begins with geometric and physical analogies:

A curve as a “path traced by a moving particle.”

Curvature as “how tightly a curve bends.”

The normal vector as “the direction in which the curve tries to turn.”

A surface as “a stretched sheet” like paper, cloth, or the Earth’s crust.

Geodesics as “the paths that feel straight when you walk them.”

Develop ideas through stories or visual imagination before introducing definitions. Prefer narrative text to lists. Build intuition as if teaching a motivated, visually minded student seeing these shapes for the first time.

Inspired by:

Bennett, Differential Geometry: A Geometric Introduction

Stewart, visual introductions to curvature

Intuition-first style of Khan Academy (adapted to higher mathematics)

