2.5 Fundamental Papers & References

At the end of every lecture, include:

🧠 Fundamental Papers & References

List 3–6 foundational sources relevant to that lecture, such as:

Gauss, Disquisitiones Generales Circa Superficies Curvas

Euler, early works on curvature

do Carmo, Differential Geometry of Curves and Surfaces

Pressley, Elementary Differential Geometry

Struik, Lectures on Classical Differential Geometry

Spivak, A Comprehensive Introduction to Differential Geometry

Use classical sources when relevant, especially for curvature, geodesics, and fundamental forms.

2.6 Applications and Cross-Disciplinary Connections

Each lecture must include:

🔗 Applications and Connections

Explain how the concept connects to:

Physics: motion of particles, curvature of trajectories, general relativity (at basic level)

Computer graphics: surface normals, shading models, curvature estimation

Robotics: orientation frames, curvature-constrained motion planning

3D modeling: surface smoothing, mesh curvature

Engineering: shell theory, stress analysis, geometric optics

Data science: manifold learning, geodesic distances

Include one concrete real-world example:

“A robot arm relies on Frenet frames to understand how its joints turn along a spatial path.”

2.7 Exercises and Reflections

Each lecture ends with:

🧩 Exercises

Focus on conceptual, geometric, and diagram-based exercises (no programming). Examples:

Sketch a plane curve and annotate its tangent and normal vectors.

Show with diagrams how the osculating circle approximates a curve locally.

Describe in words how a geodesic differs from a straight line, using the sphere as an example.

Sketch principal curvature directions on a simple surface like a cylinder.

Inspired by:

do Carmo, basic curve and surface exercises

Pressley, diagrammatic problems

Viro, intuitive elementary geometry tasks